A||||9||Rumor||I found a cool way to fix  the interior consoles!||||1||
Z||000000||Rumor||11-27-2001||02:36 AM||||I have a 1985 Fiero GT. It had some separation between the rubber and plastic parts in the interior consoles (radio and AC/heater section, Gear Shifter/power window section)Here's how I fixed them:<BR>I removed the consoles<BR>I removed the old material that was used to adhere the parts together<BR>I applied an epoxy that is meant for rubber and plastics, and crimped the parts together using a bunch of those "black metallic clip things" that hold groups of paper together.<P>It came out REALLY well - I'll post the pics on a webpage, if you're interested.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||11-27-2001||02:52 AM||||Odd that you brought that up...I just did pretty much the same thing the other day. <BR>The shift console and the console around my HVAC/radio pod was seperated BADLY. (it happens worse in the south Im convinced due to the heat)<BR>I completely seperated the material from the plastic molded piece, removed the foam around the "bad" areas, cleaned, reapplied w/epoxy. Held together w/ clamps (or whatever), VOILA! Good as new! Just remember to remove the old foam inside (not all of it, you want it to retain its "fullness".)<BR>One word of caution...I used super glue gell the first time for the shifter console. It worked great..until the next day when I went to look at it and the fumes cause it to turn white in several places. Dont use superglue, use an epoxy.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rumor||11-27-2001||03:01 AM||||I forgot to mention the crazy glue "haze".  They do sell a product that can clean that up. Any time you use Crazy glue, try to get some sort of airflow going so it doesn't cloud up on the plastic. I'd advise against the "no haze" crazy glue, I have found it just doesn't form that strong of a bond.<P>Cheers!<BR>-Rumor||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cheever3000||11-27-2001||10:06 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I'll post the pics on a webpage, if you're interested.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE>Pics!  YES!  Pics!<BR>:grunt:<BR>:snort:<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Butter||11-27-2001||11:03 AM||||I used an epoxy on mine that penetrated the foam padding. It's still together and in good shape today and has been repaired since the summer of 1999.  I used C clamps and cut a 1x4 board to fit for squeezing it back together.  <P>I cannot remember the brand name of the epoxy but I keep an extra package of it at the shop to remind me what to get.  It has an advertised strenth of 3000 psi and it sets in 15 minutes.  Along with the many things its says it will adhere too is stainless steele.  That impressed me to try it out the first time.  It did stick to stainless steele quite well by the way.  I do remember it's advertised as "plastic welding" and comes in a double tube with a connected plunger for squeezing out equal amounts to mix.<P>Somebody got a brain I can borrow?  I cannot remember the name of the stuff but I will try to find that extra package I have if anyone wants to know the brand name.  I bought my first package of it at an Advanced Auto store and have since seen the display rack at other auto stores.<P>Does anyone else get that CRS syndrome?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fformula88||11-27-2001||12:41 PM||||I used epoxy on my fathers old 87 coupe's center console.  I left the foam in as well since I couldn't remove it all, and if I removed a portion the difference in thickness would end up showing on the outside.  The repair was done about a year and a half ago and is still holding up (mycousin now has the car).  Looks good too!||||reg||1
Z||000006||recluse||11-27-2001||10:48 PM||||Epoxy is great stuff!  ||||reg||6
Z||000007||clayatandalusia||11-27-2001||10:57 PM||||i have a friend that does headliners (got mine done for cost.... 7bucks!) but anyway he had some padding for the headliners and some of the contact cement for them ... worked good... had to work fast though !!  set in like 5 mins! i'll ask him the name of that stuff.... <BR>Clay Brown <P>85' Med Royal Blue 2M4 SE 5 Speed<BR>84' White 2M4 4 Speed<BR>87' Red 2M6 5 Speed<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Marc Harris||11-28-2001||12:04 AM||||Good info, thanks. I plan to fix my '87 in three places this weekend. Glad to see there are solutions that work.<P>Marc Harris<BR>San Jose, CA<p>[This message has been edited by Marc Harris (edited 11-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||bad911||11-29-2001||11:12 PM||||Thanks for the timely info guys... I got some "Power Poxy Plastic Bonder" at Menards but haven't tried it yet. Had no success with either contact cement or my trusted 3M #77 spray. The shift-console cover is kind of tight pulling it back into place like it's shrunk a little. Did you apply the epoxy to both surfaces? Seems like it's gonna be one of those three- or four-handed jobs. <P>uh, Butter, what was the question...???<P>"Watch out for the ones that are left." <P>------------------<BR>87 2.8 AT SE<BR>85 4.9 project||||reg||1
